<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · date unknown · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">La Llorona</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A sad Mexican folk song, sung at the Day of the Dead, named after the Weeping Woman of an old ghost story.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>The people of the Isthmus of Tehuantepec, Mexico</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Isthmus of Tehuantepec, Mexico</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional Mexican folk song; verses Andres Henestrosa may have added, and later arrangements, are not)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;La Llorona&quot; (&quot;The Weeping Woman&quot;) is a Mexican folk song that takes its name and its shadow from the legend of La Llorona. In the legend she is a ghost, rather like a banshee, who drowned her children, haunts the lover who left her, and weeps for her dead children; to see her foretells death. Folklorists of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> have suggested that she is a survival of an old Mesoamerican myth of the earth that eats the dead.</p><p>The song's origins are obscure, and it exists in many versions, with scores of different verses heard on recordings. Around 1941 the composer Andres Henestrosa mentioned hearing it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-tehuantepec">Isthmus of Tehuantepec</a>. He popularised the song and may have added to its existing verses. Its sad tone keeps it from being counted a love song, and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> it is associated with the Day of the Dead. It is classed as Mexican folklore for its roots in the legend and as ranchera for its heavy use of the guitar.</p><p>Chavela Vargas recorded it in the 1950s, keeping the guitar at the centre in the ranchera manner but stripping away the trumpets and other arrangements to make a sound of her own. During the folk-song revival of the 1960s in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the guitarist Jerry Silverman published a duet arrangement in The Folksinger's Guitar Guide (1964) and recorded it in Spanish. The Spanish singer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> recorded it in the mid-1960s, the Azerbaijani singer Rashid Beibutov in the 1960s, and Joan Baez on her album Gracias a la Vida in 1974; later versions include those of Eugenia León, Lila Downs (1999), Natalia Lafourcade and Ángela Aguilar (both 2018), and Dulce Pontes in Portuguese (2006).</p><p>In the film Frida (2002), about Frida Kahlo, Chavela Vargas, who had been a close friend and frequent guest of Kahlo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-rivera">Diego Rivera</a>, appears as a ghost and sings the song while Kahlo remembers her past. The Disney-Pixar film Coco (2017) features it as a duet sung at a sunrise concert. The Guatemalan film La Llorona (2019), by Jayro Bustamante, closes with a new arrangement whose almost entirely different words leave the legend aside and turn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of the Maya-Ixil people of the Guatemalan highlands during that country's civil war, from the 1960s to the 1980s.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A lament addressed to a weeping woman, mixing love with grief and the nearness of death. One reading hears a man bound by pity to a woman who weeps whenever he thinks of leaving, until he longs for death to end his suffering; another hears a mourner singing to a wife who has died or been abandoned. Its mood is slow, sorrowful and haunted.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="1027229" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Chavela-vargas-la-llorona-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
